<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oregon Trail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1840s–1860s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oregon Trail was an overland wagon route of about 3,500 km from Independence, Missouri, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willamette-valley">Willamette Valley</a> in Oregon. From the early 1840s to the 1860s hundreds of thousands of emigrants used it and its branches to Oregon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, travelling for four to six months. It drove American settlement of the Pacific Northwest and the displacement of Native peoples along its route.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oregon-trail.jpg" alt="Oregon Trail" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wpdms_nasa_topo_oregon_trail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
